# Each part's figures put into the composed layer's unit. One row per resolved part and quantity it
# states, carrying that figure already multiplied by the part's factor; composition/fused.sqlc
# groups them by fusion afterwards.
#
# Each part converts on its own
#   Each part converts by its own single factor; nothing here mixes two parts' units. That is what
#   lets the sum that follows be a sum at all.
#
# The sign picks the end, and one rule reads both signs
#   A factor is always positive, so it can never put a claim out of order, but it does decide which
#   end each bound takes. Where the figure is not below zero the low takes the factor's low and the
#   high takes its high, which is the plain bound-by-bound product. Where the figure is below zero
#   that reverses, because more of a shortfall converted at a larger rate is the smaller number.
#   Taking the least and the greatest over both ends is one rule that reads both cases, so this
#   relation and composition/settled_remainders.sqlc convert by the same rule rather than by two.
#
#   Bound by bound alone would be right only while every figure here is at least zero, and nothing
#   ensures that: the three values are `xs:decimal` with no lower limit, and no rule is about a
#   claim's sign. A margin ratio is a subject this model admits, and a loss-making one is below
#   zero, so the figure that would break the plain product is a filing the model should accept, not
#   one it should refuse. On the corpus the two forms agree everywhere, and
#   composition/conversion_slopes.sqlc is where the end each filing took can be read.
#
# A missing factor means one, exactly
#   An omitted factor means one, exactly, and never "unknown". A part with no factor is a part whose
#   units already agree. A part whose factor is a typed blank inside the claim is a different
#   document (somebody saying nobody measured the conversion), and `coalesce` reads only the first.
#   Confusing them would quietly assert a conversion no filer made.
#
# One factor moves both figures
#   One factor multiplies both a part's nameplate and its demand, so the two converted ranges move
#   together. Subtract them as though they were independent and the range is counted twice, which
#   is why a composed remainder is converted and never worked out again from the totals.
#
# Every quantity a fusion adds up
#   One row per part and quantity, from layers/summed_quantities.sqlc: demand, nameplate and draw,
#   each converted by the same factor, and the `quantity` column says which. A part that leaves a
#   quantity unstated has no row for it here, and composition/unstated_quantities.sqlc suspends
#   that quantity's sum rather than letting the part drop out of it.
#
# A derived part is read computed
#   The figures come from composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc, so a part filing its quantity
#   `derived` enters at the figure its own fusions compute, and a part whose computation cannot
#   finish has no row, exactly like a part that leaves the quantity unstated.
#
# A typed-absent factor is not a factor of one
#   `coalesce(factor, 1)` is right only for an omitted element, which is why the last condition of
#   the join leaves out the typed blank. The two are different filings, and the schema gives them
#   different words. No `asrt:factor` at all means the part's unit and the composed layer's already
#   agree, so nothing converts and nothing is assumed. A factor filed as `unmeasured` means a
#   composer looked at a real unit crossing and could not size it; converting that by one asserts
#   the units agree, the claim they declined to make. `every-unsized-conversion/shift-capacity` is
#   that filing, and the fixture is named for it.
#
# Dropping a part is honest only where the sum is not owed
#   So the same condition is a suspension ground in `composition/unsized_conversions.sqlc`. Read the
#   two together: one removes the part, the other removes the obligation.
#
# The omitted factor is checked, not trusted
#   `checks/unit_crossing_without_a_factor` fires where the units differ and no factor is filed, so
#   an omitted element can only ever mean the units already agree.
-- asrt:Part/asrt:factor applied to each quantity the part layer states.
SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer, p.part_filing, p.part_layer, s.quantity,
       least(   s.low  * coalesce(p.factor_low, 1), s.low  * coalesce(p.factor_high, 1)) AS low,
       s.mode * coalesce(p.factor_mode, 1)                                               AS mode,
       greatest(s.high * coalesce(p.factor_low, 1), s.high * coalesce(p.factor_high, 1)) AS high
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/resolved_quantities.sqlc)
) s
       ON s.filing = p.part_filing AND s.layer = p.part_layer
  AND s.low IS NOT NULL
  AND p.factor_state IN ('omitted', 'stated')
